{"authored" [{"question" "q-342644" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nx(x-9)(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-9){y}^{′}+x(x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=3$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=-1$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=9$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\nWork over the reals. Use the classification \"outside the real coefficient domain\" at points separated from the coefficient domain. At a boundary of the coefficient domain, classify singularity by whether the normalized coefficients, or their required weighted forms, admit analytic extension from that domain." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-342644/review.json" "file_sha256" "e0dbb38d9f4ac57c81c30c499a6c3fb78e90143632deb007684b69fa9ffc6416" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-342644/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "72d4145113dc44057e9f946bb07f71c85da2374214aa8c5e3ec60bd3ecb8ddbb" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-342644/verification.json" "basis" 1995 "rationale" "Retain the original real ln(x) equation. Source categorizes-1as irregular despite lacking any real local coefficient domain there. Explicitly add the truthful outside-real-domain response and clarify boundary analyticity at0; keep source keys and raw captures unchanged. ln|x| remains only a separate reviewed recovery alternative, not asserted source intent."} {"question" "q-342644" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For the original real logarithm $\\ln x$, the coefficient domain is $x>0$. Normalize the equation on that domain away from its zero leading coefficient:\n\n$$y''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\\qquad P(x)=\\frac1{x(x+1)},\\quad Q(x)=\\frac{\\ln x}{x-9}.$$\n\nAt $x=3$, both normalized coefficients are analytic, so the point is ordinary.\n\nThe point $x=-1$ lies outside the real coefficient domain. There is no real neighborhood of $-1$ on which $\\ln x$ is defined, and it is not a boundary point approached from $x>0$. Therefore the correct classification is \"outside the real coefficient domain\". Calling it a real regular or irregular singular point would presuppose a local differential equation that is not defined there.\n\nThe point $x=0$ is a singular boundary point. From $x>0$, $xP=1/(x+1)$ has an analytic extension, but $x^2Q=x^2\\ln x/(x-9)$ does not: its second derivative has leading divergent term $-(2/9)\\ln x$. Therefore zero is irregular singular under the stated analytic extension criterion. A finite weighted-coefficient limit alone does not establish analyticity.\n\nAt $x=9$, $Q$ has a pole, while $(x-9)P$ and $(x-9)^2Q=(x-9)\\ln x$ are analytic. Therefore $x=9$ is regular singular.\n\nReplacing the original coefficient by $\\ln|x|$ would define a different equation and make $-1$ regular singular." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-342644/review.json" "file_sha256" "e0dbb38d9f4ac57c81c30c499a6c3fb78e90143632deb007684b69fa9ffc6416" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-342644/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "72d4145113dc44057e9f946bb07f71c85da2374214aa8c5e3ec60bd3ecb8ddbb" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-342644/verification.json" "basis" 1995 "rationale" "Retain the original real ln(x) equation. Source categorizes-1as irregular despite lacking any real local coefficient domain there. Explicitly add the truthful outside-real-domain response and clarify boundary analyticity at0; keep source keys and raw captures unchanged. ln|x| remains only a separate reviewed recovery alternative, not asserted source intent."} {"question" "q-342644" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "an ordinary"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-342644/review.json" "file_sha256" "e0dbb38d9f4ac57c81c30c499a6c3fb78e90143632deb007684b69fa9ffc6416" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-342644/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "72d4145113dc44057e9f946bb07f71c85da2374214aa8c5e3ec60bd3ecb8ddbb" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-342644/verification.json" "basis" 1995 "rationale" "Retain the original real ln(x) equation. Source categorizes-1as irregular despite lacking any real local coefficient domain there. Explicitly add the truthful outside-real-domain response and clarify boundary analyticity at0; keep source keys and raw captures unchanged. ln|x| remains only a separate reviewed recovery alternative, not asserted source intent."} {"question" "q-342644" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "outside the real coefficient domain"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-342644/review.json" "file_sha256" "e0dbb38d9f4ac57c81c30c499a6c3fb78e90143632deb007684b69fa9ffc6416" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-342644/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "72d4145113dc44057e9f946bb07f71c85da2374214aa8c5e3ec60bd3ecb8ddbb" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-342644/verification.json" "basis" 1995 "rationale" "Retain the original real ln(x) equation. Source categorizes-1as irregular despite lacking any real local coefficient domain there. Explicitly add the truthful outside-real-domain response and clarify boundary analyticity at0; keep source keys and raw captures unchanged. ln|x| remains only a separate reviewed recovery alternative, not asserted source intent."} {"question" "q-342644" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "an irregular singular"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-342644/review.json" "file_sha256" "e0dbb38d9f4ac57c81c30c499a6c3fb78e90143632deb007684b69fa9ffc6416" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-342644/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "72d4145113dc44057e9f946bb07f71c85da2374214aa8c5e3ec60bd3ecb8ddbb" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-342644/verification.json" "basis" 1995 "rationale" "Retain the original real ln(x) equation. Source categorizes-1as irregular despite lacking any real local coefficient domain there. Explicitly add the truthful outside-real-domain response and clarify boundary analyticity at0; keep source keys and raw captures unchanged. ln|x| remains only a separate reviewed recovery alternative, not asserted source intent."} {"question" "q-342644" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "a regular singular"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-342644/review.json" "file_sha256" "e0dbb38d9f4ac57c81c30c499a6c3fb78e90143632deb007684b69fa9ffc6416" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-342644/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "72d4145113dc44057e9f946bb07f71c85da2374214aa8c5e3ec60bd3ecb8ddbb" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-342644/verification.json" "basis" 1995 "rationale" "Retain the original real ln(x) equation. Source categorizes-1as irregular despite lacking any real local coefficient domain there. Explicitly add the truthful outside-real-domain response and clarify boundary analyticity at0; keep source keys and raw captures unchanged. ln|x| remains only a separate reviewed recovery alternative, not asserted source intent."} {"question" "q-342644" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nx(x-9)(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-9){y}^{′}+x(x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=3$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=-1$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=9$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a88534a2bb0018510bf631308f1e0c204c08fcaaa330441ecdb8cb7b45f8889" "basis" 1993 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342644" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-9)(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-9)(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-9){y}^{′}+x(x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x(x+1)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-9}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x(x+1)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-9}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x(x+1)=0,$ which occurs at $x=0$ and $x=-1.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x-9=0$ or where $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is undefined. These occur at $x=9$ and $x\\le 0.$\n\nTherefore, $x=3$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,x=0,\\,x=9.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)P(x)=(x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-1,$ but the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{2}Q(x)=(x+1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-9},\n$$\n\nis undefined about $x=-1,$ so is *not* analytic about $x=-1.$ Thus, $x=-1$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x+1},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ but the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-9},\n$$\n\nis undefined about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=9,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-9)P(x)=(x-9)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=9,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-9)^{2}Q(x)=(x-9)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-9}=(x-9)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=9.$ Therefore, $x=9$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a88534a2bb0018510bf631308f1e0c204c08fcaaa330441ecdb8cb7b45f8889" "basis" 1993 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342644" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9d5a40b41cd7c35c2649bce99a7377e8bdf4672a1b9f3e346072a18cee354131" "basis" 1993 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342644" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a88534a2bb0018510bf631308f1e0c204c08fcaaa330441ecdb8cb7b45f8889" "basis" 1993 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342644" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "a regular singular"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a88534a2bb0018510bf631308f1e0c204c08fcaaa330441ecdb8cb7b45f8889" "basis" 1993 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342644" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "an irregular singular"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a88534a2bb0018510bf631308f1e0c204c08fcaaa3